  "problem": "The center of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-36x+50y-1=0$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-36x+50y-1 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-36x)+(5{y}^{2}+50y)-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{x}^{2}-36x)+(5{y}^{2}+50y)-1 & =0 \\\\ 6[{x}^{2}-6x]+5[{y}^{2}+10y]-1 & =0 \\\\ 6[(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}]+5[(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]-1 & =0 \\\\ 6[(x-3)^{2}-9]+5[(y+5)^{2}-25]-1 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-3)^{2}-54+5(y+5)^{2}-125-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x-3)^{2}-54+5(y+5)^{2}-125-1 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-3)^{2}+5(y+5)^{2} & =1+54+125 \\\\ 6(x-3)^{2}+5(y+5)^{2} & =180 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{30}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{30})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-5).$",
